  "title": "# Breakroom HR: automated, fun, cheap (Brett, Oct 31 night) Read CLAUDE.md, _claude/shared/rules/RU…",
  "asked": "# Breakroom HR: automated, fun, cheap (Brett, Oct 31 night) Read CLAUDE.md, _claude/shared/rules/RULES.md, _claude/persona.md, then the agent swarm on main: apps/console-web/plugins/agent-swarm (+ README, report _claude/builds/reports/agent-swarm-2026-10-31.md, install_steps Part I). Opus, one session. Git login works: plain `git push`. Wait for nothing; if banner-fix or leftovers touch the same files, merge origin/main before pushing. Brett wants a whole Human Resources department for the agent swarm: lots of rules, totally automated, really fun. Firing, layoffs, probation, write-ups, raises, all handled by the system. ## The cost rule (most important) HR rules must NOT be read into any agent's context by default. They cost tokens. - Rules live as hot-swappable assets (new asset type hr-rule@1 in framework/schemas + validators, grouped in an hr-handbook pack), evaluated by plain code (an HR engine in the swarm plugin), never by an LLM. - Triggers are events the swarm already has: failed run, test break, broken hook/check, lease violation, over token budget, push to main red, rage-quit, idle, streaks of green builds, fast finishes, praise from Boss, etc. - An agent only gets HR text when it matters to its next task: one short line (e.g. 'You are on probation: 2 more red builds = fired'), max ~60 tokens, injected only for agents with an active HR status. Never the handbook. - Measure it: a test proves a normal agent run gets 0 HR tokens. ## Make lots of rules (40+), fun ones Write them as assets with a funny name, plain-English description, trigger, conditions, consequence and a chat line. Mix of: - Discipline ladder: verbal warning -> written write-up -> probation -> final warning -> fired (pink slip). Auto-escalates, records expire after a clean streak. - Layoffs when the weekly token budget runs hot (last hired, first fired, or lowest output), with severance = a small budget refund, and 'rehire eligible' flags. - Breaking a hook/check or leaving ghosts = immediate probation ('Ghostbuster violation'). - Rewards: employee of the week, raises, bonuses, promotions to senior/lead (bigger budget, harder tasks), corner office (priority in the queue). - Fun stuff: exit interviews (one roast line, auto-generated from templates, no LLM), HR memos posted to the group chat by an 'HR' bot persona, mandatory fun Fridays, desk cleanups, the 'it works on my machine' clause, coffee-machine privileges, rage-quit cooling-off period, rehire with a probation badge, a hall of shame and a hall of fame, an HR complaints box Boss can drop notes into. - Every action shows on the agent's ID card (badge/stamp) and in the agent's history. - Tone: Amber-style banter and roasts about WORK only. No identity jokes, no slurs, no sexual stuff. ## UI An HR tab in the Breakroom: the handbook (searchable, framework grid, rules editable as assets with a toggle each), HR cases (who, what rule, when, status), hall of fame/shame, HR settings (strictness slider, layoffs on/off, auto-fire on/off; default: everything automated). Framework controls + theme/scale tokens only. Boss (Brett) can overrule any action with one tap (pardon, rehire, fire anyway). ## Done means Tests: engine fires the right consequences per event, escalation ladder, expiry, layoffs math, 0 HR tokens for clean agents, the one-line injection only when status is active, overrule works. Full local test block from .github/workflows/android.yml + theme/controls/lifecycle checks. Phone + desktop screenshots to UI Review via framework/cli/shot.mjs, group 'Breakroom HR - Oct 31 night'. Add 'Part J: HR department' to install_steps. Commit, fetch+merge origin/main, retest, `git push origin HEAD:main` (never force). Report at _claude/builds/reports/swarm-hr.md with the full rule list in plain English. Watch live: Brett can watch the screen you are changing hot-render in the console. Whenever you start work on a screen or element, run `node framework/cli/axf.mjs focus <app> [route] [css-selector] --note \"<wh…",
